<section class="inner-sec">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">How it works</div>
  <h2>Every call answered, every pet's details captured</h2>
  <p class="lede">One receptionist covers the phone, the texts, the website and the socials — capturing what you actually need to know about the animal before the conversation ever reaches you.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Missed-call text-back</strong>A call you couldn't take mid-groom becomes a text conversation, instead of a voicemail you clear after closing.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Gets the pet's details first</strong>Breed, size, coat condition, age, temperament, and what the owner is actually asking for — captured before you call back.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Never quotes blind</strong>It explains how you price and what changes it — matting, size, coat type — then tells them you'll confirm. No number goes out that you didn't set.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Screens vaccination requirements</strong>The single most repeated question in boarding and daycare, answered instantly from the policy you've published.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Books and rebooks</strong>New appointments onto your calendar, and a nudge when a regular is due for their next groom, so the six-week cycle doesn't quietly become twelve.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Human handoff</strong>Anything about an animal's health, an unusual request, or a distressed owner goes to you immediately with a summary.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">Who it's built for</div>
  <h2>Wherever the phone competes with the animal</h2>
  <p class="lede">Grooming, boarding and veterinary front desks look different, but they share one problem: the person who should answer is holding a pet.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Grooming salons</strong>Every quote depends on the animal in front of you, and every call arrives while your hands are full of one.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Mobile groomers</strong>You're driving between appointments or working in the van. There is no front desk, and there never will be.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Boarding kennels</strong>Seasonal demand arrives in waves. The holiday enquiry that reaches voicemail is a two-week booking lost to the next kennel.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Doggy daycare</strong>High volume, repetitive questions — hours, requirements, trial days, temperament testing — and a team that's outside with the dogs.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Veterinary practices</strong>New-client calls, prescription and appointment requests, and after-hours callers who need routing to emergency care fast and correctly.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Solo &amp; small teams</strong>One or two people doing the work and the admin. Every answered call is one that didn't interrupt a groom.</div>
  </div>
</section>

